---
import JapanServicePage from "@components/japan/JapanServicePage.astro";
import type { Article } from "@components/japan/JapanServicePage.astro";
import { createPageSeo } from "@lib/seo/seo-config";
import { createBreadcrumbs } from "@lib/breadcrumbs/breadcrumbs";
import { usd, countryOgImage } from "@data/countries/international";
import { JPY_PER_USD, jpy, both, ja, japanRegions, japanServiceSchema } from "@data/countries/japan";

const path = "/japan/ai-automation-engineer/";

// ---------- FAQ (visible list and FAQPage schema share this array; plain text only) ----------

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "How much does AI automation cost in Japan?",
    answer: `2026 price guides from Japanese agencies and comparison sites put business automation projects at roughly ¥200,000 to ¥1,000,000, AI chatbot or AI-OCR systems at ¥500,000 to ¥3,000,000 initially, and fully custom AI development at ¥1,000,000 to ¥5,000,000 or more. Our AI automation work starts from ${both("AI automation")} for one mapped workflow, and automations that need their own screens start from ${both("Custom web app")}. Running costs such as model usage and platform plans are extra and paid directly to the providers. The final quote depends on your documents, systems and accuracy needs.`,
  },
  {
    question: "How much does it cost to develop an AI chatbot in Japan?",
    answer: `Japanese price guides quote about ¥50,000 to ¥150,000 for a minimal FAQ bot, ¥150,000 to ¥400,000 for a basic AI chatbot, ¥400,000 to ¥800,000 for a complex one and more than ¥800,000 for very complex builds, while hosted chatbot services can cost ¥100,000 to ¥300,000 or more a month. A template FAQ bot on a SaaS tool can cost less than our ${both("AI automation")} starting price. Ours makes sense when the chatbot must answer from your own documents and data, hand over to staff and connect to your systems.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Can an AI chatbot answer customers in natural, polite Japanese?",
    answer: "Yes, current language models write Japanese well, including keigo, but tone still needs control. We build the assistant from a style guide and model answers approved by your team: how you address customers, your standard phrases and the words to avoid. A native speaker on your side reviews sample conversations during testing. Nobody on our team writes natural Japanese, which is why Japanese wording is never signed off by us. Replies that commit your business, such as prices, refunds or delivery promises, are drafted for staff approval rather than sent automatically.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do your engineers speak Japanese?",
    answer: "No. We work in English and do not write natural Japanese. That does not stop us building Japanese-language systems: we handle the engineering, including Japanese text processing, OCR of Japanese documents, search with morphological analysis and prompt design, while the Japanese wording is written or approved by you, a bilingual colleague or a translator. Projects work well with one English-reading person on your side who joins our chat and relays feedback. Every decision is written down so colleagues can review it in translation.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can you build AI search over our internal documents in Japanese?",
    answer: "Yes. We build retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) systems that answer staff questions from your manuals, rules, specifications and past proposals, with a link to the source passage for every answer. For Japanese we split documents along their headings and sentence boundaries, combine meaning-based search with keyword search using Japanese morphological analysis, and filter results by each user's existing access rights. Before launch we measure accuracy on a set of real questions from your staff. If the documents do not contain an answer, the assistant says so instead of guessing.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can AI-OCR read FAX orders and handwritten forms?",
    answer: "It reads clear printed FAX and PDF orders reliably, including many different customer layouts without a template for each. Faint, skewed or heavily handwritten documents are harder, and no system reads them perfectly. That is why our pipeline validates every extracted field against your customer and product masters and sends uncertain orders to a review screen that shows the original image. Before going live we test on your real samples and tell you what share of orders pass without correction, so you decide with real numbers.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can you build a LINE Official Account chatbot for our business?",
    answer: `Yes. Using the LINE Messaging API we build booking and reminder flows, order-status answers, rich menus, LINE Login linked to your customer records and AI assistants that answer common questions from your information and hand over to staff in the LINE chat. The LINE account and channel stay in your company's name, and the monthly LINE plan is paid by you to LY Corporation. Since LINE Notify ended in March 2025, we also move internal alerts to the Messaging API, Chatwork, Slack or Teams. LINE work starts from ${both("AI automation")}.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Do you integrate with kintone, freee, Money Forward Cloud and Chatwork?",
    answer: "Yes, through their official APIs and webhooks wherever they are available. Typical flows are AI-OCR orders written to a kintone app with a Chatwork alert, invoice data prepared in freee or Money Forward Cloud for your accountant to approve, and daily summaries posted to a chat room. We also connect Slack, Microsoft Teams, Google Workspace, Microsoft 365, Salesforce, HubSpot and Shopify. Where an older system has no API, we can keep an existing RPA robot for the final entry step and put AI in front of it.",
  },
  {
    question: "Will our data be used to train AI models?",
    answer: "The business and API terms of the major model providers generally state that data sent through their APIs is not used to train their models by default, but terms differ between providers and change over time, so we review the current terms for the provider you choose before the project starts. The account is opened in your company's name, so the contract is between you and the provider. We also minimise what reaches the model at all, masking personal data whenever a task does not need it.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can the AI system run on servers in Japan?",
    answer: "The application side can. AWS, Google Cloud and Microsoft Azure all have regions in Tokyo and Osaka, so the workflow, database, document index and logs can run in Japan with backups in the other region. For the model itself, options include Azure OpenAI deployments in Japan East and Amazon Bedrock's Japan cross-Region inference for supported models, but availability changes often, so we confirm current options at the start. If data must stay in Japan, we choose a deployment that meets that requirement and explain any trade-off.",
  },
  {
    question: "Are there AI laws in Japan that affect our automation?",
    answer: "Japan has chosen a light approach so far. The AI Promotion Act, passed in May 2025 and fully in effect since September 2025, promotes AI and lets the government issue guidance and request cooperation, but it has no fines. The AI Guidelines for Business from METI and the Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications are voluntary. Existing laws still apply in full: the APPI for personal data, the Copyright Act, advertising rules for what an assistant claims, and sector rules in healthcare and finance. This is background, not legal advice.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is it legal under the APPI for your team in India to handle our data?",
    answer: "It can be, with the right basis, but your adviser should confirm it. If our team can access personal data you hold, the APPI regards it as giving data to a third party located outside Japan. That normally requires informed consent from the people concerned, or a recipient whose protection measures match the APPI standard. India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 is being phased in. We design projects to avoid live personal data wherever possible, use dummy or masked data, keep production access minimal and sign data-handling terms. This is not legal advice.",
  },
  {
    question: "Will your invoices include Japanese consumption tax, and are they qualified invoices?",
    answer: "Neither. Our team works from India and holds no registration as a qualified invoice issuer under Japan's invoice system. That means no Japanese consumption tax appears on our bills, and your accounts team should not treat them as qualified invoices when claiming credits. For AI and software services bought from overseas, the correct tax handling, including any withholding, turns on the type of service and your company's position, so ask your zeirishi before the first transfer. We send itemised invoices and our business details to make that check easy.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can we pay in yen, and how do payments work?",
    answer: `Yes. We publish prices in US dollars with approximate yen at ¥${JPY_PER_USD} per dollar, and on request we invoice in JPY with the amount fixed in the written quote, so exchange rate movements during the project do not change your bill. Payment is by Wise or international bank wire. Payments follow milestones: an advance to start, a payment when you approve the workflow design or prototype, and the balance before go-live. Third-party costs such as API usage are paid by you directly to the providers.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Who pays for OpenAI, LINE, n8n or Zapier costs?",
    answer: "You do, directly to each provider. Model API usage, cloud hosting, LINE Official Account plans, Zapier, Make or n8n subscriptions, internet FAX or OCR services and extra SaaS seats are all billed to your company's accounts. We do not resell them or add a margin. The quote includes an estimate of monthly running costs based on your volumes and sample documents, and we set usage alerts and budgets so costs cannot grow unnoticed. We also design to keep them low, for example by using smaller models for simple steps.",
  },
  {
    question: "How long does an AI automation project take?",
    answer: "One well-defined workflow usually takes two to four weeks from approved scope to go-live, followed by a parallel run of one to two weeks while staff check every output. Automations that need their own review screens or admin panel follow the custom web app timeline of six to twelve weeks. The biggest factors are how quickly we receive sample documents and system access, how many exceptions the process has, and how many people inside your company need to approve the result.",
  },
  {
    question: "What are your working hours in Japan time?",
    answer: "We are online from 9 am to 9 pm IST every day, which in Japan means from half past noon until half past midnight, reachable on WhatsApp throughout. The gap between IST and JST stays fixed at three and a half hours because neither country moves its clocks. Calls sit comfortably in your afternoon, and if you need an earlier slot we can book a 10 am JST call ahead of time. Go-live and first-day monitoring are scheduled in your afternoon while we are online and your staff are still at work.",
  },
  {
    question: "Who owns the code, prompts and workflows?",
    answer: "You do. Code repositories, prompts, test sets, workflow definitions, cloud accounts, model provider accounts, automation platform accounts and the LINE channel are in your company's name or transferred to you at handover, together with every login. We document how the automation works in English so another developer could take it over. We do not hold systems hostage for support contracts, and you are free to maintain the automation yourself or with another vendor later.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can AI replace our RPA robots such as WinActor or UiPath?",
    answer: "Sometimes, and sometimes it should work alongside them. Desktop RPA follows fixed screen steps and cannot read unstructured documents; AI can read FAXes, emails and PDFs but should not click through screens blindly. Where your systems have APIs, a direct integration with an AI reading step is usually sturdier than a robot. Where a legacy system has no API, we keep the robot for the final entry and put AI in front of it. We look at your existing scenarios before recommending either approach.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can an IT subsidy pay for our AI automation project?",
    answer: "Unlikely, if you work with us. Japan's IT adoption subsidy programmes, such as the IT Introduction Subsidy and its successors, generally only fund tools supplied through registered IT support providers, and we hold no such registration. If subsidy funding is essential for your budget, a registered Japanese vendor is the practical choice. Other subsidy programmes have their own rules about overseas providers, so confirm eligibility with the programme office or your chamber of commerce before you commit to any provider, us included.",
  },
  {
    question: "What can AI automation not do reliably?",
    answer: "It cannot guarantee perfect accuracy, especially on poor scans and handwriting. Without grounding in your documents, a language model can produce confident but wrong answers. Models change when providers update or retire them, and costs grow with volume. AI also does not fix a confused process; it only makes the confusion faster. Some decisions, such as hiring, credit and medical advice, should stay with people. We build validation, review queues, citations and test sets precisely because of these limits.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do we start an AI automation project with you?",
    answer: "Send us one process in English on WhatsApp or email: what arrives, what your staff do with it, how many items per day or month and which systems are involved. Two or three anonymised samples help a lot. We reply with questions, then a written scope, a running-cost estimate and a quote in USD or JPY. If the numbers show the process is not worth automating, we will tell you. Nothing is built or billed until you sign off the scope.",
  },
];

